---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Ninety hollow dots strung along a line toward a single square, one of them filled gold, drawn as a two-ink risograph print: a queue and the one server it is waiting for"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="thesis">There is no neutral queue.</p>

  <p>
    Every line you have ever stood in was running a rule that decided who went
    first. Somebody chose that rule — or, more often, inherited it from whoever
    laid out the room and never looked at it again. It is deciding whose time is
    worth less, and it is doing that without saying so, to people who have no
    way to bill anyone for the hours.
  </p>

  <p>
    This course is twelve weeks on how queues form, what waiting costs, who pays
    for it, and what changes when you change the rule. It is not a queueing
    theory unit, though you will have the core of the mathematics by week 3 and
    it takes about forty minutes. The mathematics answers <em>how long</em>. The
    interesting question is <em>who</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Measure real queues, with a stopwatch, in person — starting in the first
    lab and continuing all semester. Build simulations and break them. Board a
    taped-out aircraft cabin four different ways and time it. Triage forty case
    cards under pressure and then find the pattern in your own errors.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will finish with a design brief for one real queue: diagnosed with
    evidence you gathered, redesigned to the level of an ordering rule and its
    exceptions, costed honestly for whoever ends up worse off, and defended
    against the strongest version of the alternative you rejected.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone in second year or beyond. No prerequisites, no prior statistics, no
    programming required — the simulation work can be done in a spreadsheet, on
    paper, or with the course simulator, and several people every year do it
    with dice. What it asks for instead is a willingness to stand in a car park
    for forty minutes counting people, and to accept that most of the queues you
    are about to look at are somebody's unexamined decision rather than a fact
    of nature.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="The shape of the semester" href="/arc/">
      <p>
        The three acts, the twelve ideas, and the chain showing what each week
        stands on. Start here if you want to know whether it holds together.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Twelve weeks, four of them carrying a simulation you can drive yourself.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly fieldwork: what to bring, what happens, what leaves the room.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces — measure, reorder, simulate, redesign. What each is worth and when.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .thesis {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--at-primary);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.75rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .thesis { font-size: 2rem; }
  }
</style>
